docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:3:> **Claim wording after the ARIS result-to-claim review.** All three claims were rated *partial*, with
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:4:> high confidence. The reviewer's revised wordings ([aris/m3_RESULT_TO_CLAIM.md](aris/m3_RESULT_TO_CLAIM.md))
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:5:> are the claims of record. P2 is a validity-power trade-off shown empirically on the tested Markov
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:6:> family; it is not a theorem. P1 is a scoped, synthetic-only method result. Calling either one a
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:7:> "main-track candidate" is a publication judgement, not an experimental result.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:11:All numbers come from the sprint validation files in [validation/](validation/). Status uses three values: PROMISING, NEEDS EVIDENCE and NOT SUPPORTED. The result-to-claim review in [aris/](aris/) may revise these.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:18:* **Insight / mechanism.**
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:27:* **Evidence.** All results are on fresh seeds, after the DISCERN `/4` fix ([transfer_results.md](validation/transfer_results.md), [rmm_certified_hosts.txt](validation/rmm_certified_hosts.txt)).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:32:  * Independent-null first-promotion safety holds on all three hosts (Wilson upper bound ≤ .0936 at 1000 seeds).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:33:  * Late improvement vs the best certified published method (Xu) is −15 to −27 per episode for every certified host, on V_late_600 and W_late_1000.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:36:* **Adverse results.**
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:37:  * Registered HR fails at 3/5 hosts. Model Picker and CAMS have no certificate for RMM to improve: their null promotion is .94 to .95 with or without RMM.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:39:  * Strong stationary improvement costs +9 to +34 vs front-loaded EB.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:46:  * Reversal is +3 to +35 for 3 of 4 hosts, and ρ=.8/.95 null promotion is .12–.18 / ≈.71, as declared in advance.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:47:  * This turns the earlier post-hoc certificate-host scope into a registered result.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:61:* **Research question.** Do anytime-valid deployment gates keep their false-promotion guarantee when label outcomes are serially dependent, as they are in real time-series streams? What does a dependence-robust certificate cost?
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:63:  * Every certified published port that has power exceeds the .0936 bound on a persistent marginal null. Run at 1000 seeds each:
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:68:    * Choe–Ramdas-style EB: ρ=.95 .213, front-loaded ρ=.8 .116
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:71:  * A gap-indexed sequential-universal-inference certificate (A1b/A5b) holds the bound for every ρ ≤ .95. It also holds on two out-of-class nulls (.048, .018). Its stationary cost is within +7.4 of the cheapest unsafe certified method.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:72:  * But it loses all power on real time-series streams. Fresh real data: +61 to +257 vs the best certified method. On metro it costs +99 with 0/16 episodes better, even though the challenger error is .047 vs .36.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:74:  * This sits alongside the shared certification-latency diagnosis (M1, uniform pacing under a binding budget) and the churn of uncertified selectors (M2, 64 to 100% null promotion).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:80:  * The atlas measures marginal false promotion of deployment gates under controlled persistence, on one common budgeted, delayed simulator, against 13 published methods. It also shows the conditional-vs-marginal null distinction has decision consequences.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:96:  * Native null promotion of 64 to 100% falls to .014 to .016 on V_null for all three hosts at 1000 seeds.
