===== docs/sota_sprint/validation_protocol.md =====
     1	# SOTA sprint: locked validation protocol
     2	
     3	Locked 2026-11-03, after the 24-candidate development screen and before any validation run. The
     4	validation regimes and seeds were registered earlier in `scripts/sota_sprint/validation_spec.py`
     5	(commit 39d25ce, before any family reported). Nothing below may be changed after the first
     6	validation row is written; deviations are reported as deviations.
     7	
     8	## Seeds and data
     9	* Synthetic: seed base 2026110601, 100 paired seeds per regime, regimes `VAL_REGIMES` (13 Atlas v2
    10	  V_* instances, which had only been run with base 2026110399, plus 8 new W_* instances).
    11	* Null safety: seed base 2026110602, 1000 seeds on each regime in `NULL_REGIMES`
    12	  (V_null, V_worse, V_null_dep_r5, V_null_dep_r8, W_null_dep_r9, W_null_dep_r95, W_worse_dep_r8).
    13	* Real data (secondary, descriptive): the nine cached streams with episode-level inference and 20
    14	  policy seeds at base 2026110603. These streams were exposed in Atlas v2 development; no real-data
    15	  SOTA claim can be made from them.
    16	
    17	## Advanced candidates (at most two per family, chosen from development evidence)
    18	| family | candidates | host / control |
    19	|---|---|---|
    20	| A | A5b, A1b | QSG; A1b is A5b without adaptive density |
    21	| B | B2 | rmm:gated_deployment+RMM |
    22	| C | C2, C6 | gated_adaptive_range+front, gated_adaptive_range+pace_tuned |
    23	| D | D1, D2 | model_picker_adapter, cams_adapter |
    24	| combinations | chosen from the combination screen by the rule in H4 | as in the combination ledger |
    25	
    26	## Comparators
    27	* Certified published (`bench.CERTIFIED_PUBLISHED`): gated_deployment, gated_adaptive_range,
    28	  gated_adaptive_range+front, discern_style (corrected), discern_design_floor,
    29	  SMCS+retain-incumbent, xu_acquisition_only. Also the Choe-Ramdas-style EB comparators
    30	  choe_ramdas_eb and choe_ramdas_eb+front, which are added to the certified class for every
    31	  validation contrast.
    32	* Uncertified published: model_picker_adapter, model_selector_causal, online_llm_picker,
    33	  cams_adapter, variance_min_ipw, gong_zhang_paid_obs, qiu_delayed_movement, ryabchenko_capacity.
    34	* Internal (never "SOTA"): QSG, QSG-env8, gated_deployment+QCE+front, RMM, LRMD, P7s. Control:
    35	  never-switch, economic_replacement.
    36	* "Strongest applicable comparator" = the comparator in the stated class with the lowest mean cost
    37	  on that regime on the same seeds (selected after the fact, conservative for the candidate).
    38	
    39	## Hypotheses
    40	**H1, primary (reliability at matched cost; A5b).** All three parts must hold:
    41	1. (Safety) A5b's promotion rate is <= .0936 on every NULL_REGIMES regime over 1000 seeds.
    42	2. (Comparator failure) Every certified published comparator, including both Choe-Ramdas-style
    43	   ports, has a promotion rate above .0936 with Wilson 95% lower bound above .05 on at least one
    44	   NULL_REGIMES regime. Otherwise H1 is restated against the comparators that pass.
    45	3. (Non-inferior cost) On the iid stationary alternatives {V_better_a, V_better_b, V_better_c,
    46	   V_near, W_delay600_better}, the upper 95% bound of J(A5b) - J(strongest certified published)
    47	   is <= +5 on at least 4 of 5 regimes. The margin is 5 cost units per 2000 events, about 1.3% of
    48	   cost.
    49	   
    50	   Known dev losses are declared and reported but are not part of H1: V_bursty; dependent
    51	   alternatives V_dep_better_r5, W_better_dep_r7, W_better_dep_r9; change regimes V_late_600,
    52	   W_late_1000, V_reversal_1000, W_reversal_700, V_recurrent.
    53	
    54	   If H1 holds, the claim is: "the only method in the comparison whose false-promotion rate stays
    55	   within the registered bound under persistence up to rho = .95, at cost non-inferior to the
    56	   strongest certified published method on iid stationary alternatives."
    57	
    58	**H2 (cost superiority; secondary).** Holm-adjusted paired Delta J < 0 against the strongest
    59	certified published comparator, over the 5 iid stationary regimes above, for A5b, D1, D2, C2 and
    60	C6 (one Holm family per candidate). A cost superiority claim for a candidate is admissible only if
    61	that candidate also meets H1-part-1 safety; otherwise it is reported as "cheaper but unsafe".
    62	
    63	**H3 (mechanism attribution).** For each of A5b vs A1b (adaptive density), B2 vs its host on
    64	V_reversal_1000 and W_reversal_700 (rollback), and D1 vs model_picker_adapter on nulls, report the
    65	paired difference; this is descriptive.
    66	
    67	**H4 (combinations).** A combination advances to validation only if, on the 20-seed combination
    68	screen, its 95% CI vs its parent is below 0 on at least one targeted regime, with no null regime
    69	promotion above 2/20. Advanced combinations are tested under H1/H2 exactly as A5b.
    70	
    71	## Verdict rule
    72	`SOTA_MET` requires H1 (or H2 with safety) for at least one candidate on fresh synthetic seeds,
    73	plus a successful independent result-to-claim review of that exact claim. Real-data evidence on
    74	exposed streams is descriptive. The task's success gate requires independent real-data validation
    75	for a real-data claim, so any SOTA verdict is scoped "synthetic, under the declared Markov
    76	dependence class".
    77	
    78	## Amendment 1 (2026-11-03, before any validation row was read)
    79	A strict re-read of the brief (Phase 6: "at least the strongest two candidates per family should advance
    80	where functioning candidates exist") found Family B had only one advanced candidate. B5 (largest
    81	reversal gain vs host in development) is added as B's second candidate. It runs in a supplemental job on
    82	the same validation seed bases and regimes (streams are keyed by seed base, regime and seed, so rows pair
    83	with the main run by seed). It is evaluated under H3 (vs host on change regimes) and the H1-part-1 safety
    84	check. The two other changes are bookkeeping: the gate re-screen of all 24 at the committed revision
    85	(Phase 5 traceability) and the fresh real-data protocol (`fresh_real_protocol.md`, external validation).
    86	
    87	## Amendment 2 (2026-11-03, before any validation row was read)
    88	This amendment responds to the milestone 1-2 review (`review_milestone12.md`). The lead confirms that no
    89	validation record, log line or summary had been opened when it was committed. It supersedes H1 and H2
    90	where they conflict. Every original metric is still reported.
    91	
    92	* **O1 (stop rule carried over; claim wording).**
    93	  * **H0 restores the Atlas v2 registered stop rule verbatim** (Atlas v2 report section 9):
    94	    * Test a persistence-robust certified variant (A5b; A1b as its density-off control) against
    95	      xu_acquisition_only and tuned non-uniform pacing (gated_adaptive_range+pace_tuned).
    96	    * Success requires promotion <= .094 at rho=.8 and rho=.95, and paired CI < 0 against the stronger
    97	      of the two baselines on >= 4 of the 6 stationary alternatives {V_better_a, V_better_b, V_better_c,
    98	      V_dep_better_r5, V_near, V_bursty}. **If H0 fails, no cost-SOTA claim is made for A5b.**
    99	  * The H1 claim no longer says "only".
   100	  * H1 part 2 is replaced by a Pareto statement. Every certified published comparator must either
   101	    (a) fail a NULL_REGIMES null, meaning point rate > .0936 and Wilson 95% lower bound > .05, or
   102	    (b) be cost-dominated by A5b, meaning the 95% upper bound of J(A5b) - J(comparator) is < 0 on
   103	    >= 3 of the 6 stationary alternatives above.
   104	  * Every comparator is listed with its status.
   105	* **O2 (class dependence).**
   106	  * Any safety claim names the class: a stationary first-order homogeneous Markov sign in event time,
   107	    with p <= 1/2 and any rho, independent of disagreement arrivals, under constant delay.
   108	  * Two out-of-class nulls (`ooc_regimes.py`) run descriptively at 1000 seeds, base 2026110605, for
   109	    every candidate and certified comparator. They are reported next to the in-class nulls; they are
   110	    not part of H1.
   111	* **O3 (bursty).**
   112	  * Part 3 is also reported as k/6 including V_bursty.
   113	  * If A5b is not non-inferior on V_bursty, the claim is restricted to Bernoulli (non-bursty)
   114	    disagreement arrivals.
   115	  * The claim states that A5b has near-zero power against persistent alternatives.
   116	* **O4 (harm).** Every null reports harm: mean J minus never-switch J. Promotion rate alone is not
   117	  treated as harm.
   118	* **O5 (comparators).**
   119	  * choe_ramdas_eb is documented as the query-conditional PrPl-EB port (= DiscernQCE); it is not
   120	    author code.
   121	  * The betting-CS query-conditional ports gated_deployment+QCE and gated_deployment+QCE+front join the
   122	    certified comparator class as a supplemental run on the same seeds.
   123	  * The IPW betting form is discern_style/gated_adaptive_range (already in the class).
   124	  * Waudby-Smith et al. 2022 is NOT implemented, a recorded gap.
   125	  * RESULTS.md's statement about rho=.95 is corrected.
   126	* **O6 (X_A5C).** X_A5C may be claimed only if it beats A5b (paired CI < 0) on its targeted regimes
   127	  V_late_600 and W_late_1000.
   128	* **O8.** H1 part 1 uses the Wilson 95% upper bound <= .0936, not the point estimate.
   129	* **O9.**
   130	  * H1 parts 1/3 and H2 are evaluated for X_A5C and B5 as well.
   131	  * H2 success for a candidate = Holm-adjusted p < .05 on >= 4 of 5 iid alternatives AND H1 part 1
   132	    safety.
   133	* **O10, O12, O13, O14 (recorded caveats).**
   134	  * O10: the Family C held-out seeds share the null-check base and were used to pick C6.
   135	  * O12: four of the five part-3 regimes were exposed in Atlas v2, although with other seeds.
   136	  * O13: B2 and Family C constants come from generator design knowledge.
   137	  * O14: D's host-AND is inert.
   138	* **O11.** B5 is advanced only to satisfy Phase 6's two-per-family requirement; Family B recommended
   139	  stopping it.
   140	
   141	## Amendment 3 (2026-11-03): RMM transferable-extension test (publishability addendum)
   142	
   143	Registered before any sprint validation row was read. The test uses fresh seeds, so it is independent
   144	of the sprint validation rows. Every earlier RMM result predates the DISCERN PrPl-EB `/4` fix. The
   145	`rmm:discern_style+RMM` factory now wraps the corrected `DiscernStyleStop`.
   146	
   147	**HR (transfer).**
   148	* Hosts: gated_deployment, discern_style (corrected), SMCS+retain-incumbent, model_picker_adapter and
   149	  cams_adapter, each also run with RMM attached.
   150	* Primary family, the change-and-stationary-improvement regimes the RMM claim was made on:
   151	  {V_late_600, W_late_1000, V_better_a, V_better_b, V_better_c, V_near}.
   152	* Statistic: the per-seed family mean of J(h+RMM) - J(h).
   153	* Seeds: 100 fresh synthetic seeds, base 2026110606.
   154	* HR passes if the 95% CI is below 0 for at least 4 of the 5 hosts, and first-promotion safety holds
   155	  under the iid conditional null. Safety means the Wilson upper bound is at most .0936 on V_null and
   156	  V_worse over 1000 seeds (base 2026110607).
   157	* The ρ=.8/.95 Markov nulls are reported as known out-of-scope failures. RMM's validity is stated
   158	  under the iid conditional null only.
   159	
   160	**HR2 (versus strong baselines, descriptive plus one test).** The same family mean is reported for the
   161	best host+RMM against:
   162	* gated_adaptive_range+front
   163	* gated_deployment+QCE+front
   164	* xu_acquisition_only
   165	* discern_design_floor
   166	* P7s_settled
   167	* A5b
   168	* the best certified published comparator, chosen per regime
   169	
   170	The one test asks whether the best host+RMM is significantly cheaper than the best certified published
   171	comparator on {V_late_600, W_late_1000}. Holm correction is applied over the two regimes.
   172	
   173	**Attribution.**
   174	* +RMM_single, which drops the restart mixture.
   175	* +RMM_noacq, which drops continued acquisition.
   176	
   177	## Publication track
   178	The publishability addendum (2026-11-03) keeps two separate assessments, main-track contribution
   179	potential and SOTA performance. They are recorded in `publication_register.md` and in the leaderboard.
   180	They do not change any registered hypothesis above.
   181	
   182	## Amendment 4 (2026-11-03): transfer of the D deploy-gate (registered after reading the validation results)
   183	
   184	This test is registered **after** the sprint validation and real-data results were read: on the fresh real
   185	streams, D2 (CAMS plus the gate) was the only candidate that beat the best certified published comparator
   186	on most datasets. It is therefore a confirmation test of that observation. It runs on new synthetic seeds
   187	and on the fresh real streams.
   188	
   189	**HD (transfer of one gate across three ranking hosts).**
   190	* The gate uses D1's parameter set. It is attached to three hosts: Model Picker (`D1`), CAMS (`D2`) and
   191	  Model Selector (`D6-D1params:ms`).
   192	* For each host, these quantities are computed:
   193	  * (i) J(gated) - J(native host), paired, over all VAL regimes, the nulls included.
   194	  * (ii) J(gated) - J(best certified published), on the 5 iid alternatives, Holm-corrected.
   195	  * (iii) false promotion on the iid nulls V_null and V_worse, plus V_null_dep_r5, from 1000 fresh seeds.
   196	* Seeds: synthetic 100 at base 2026110608; nulls 1000 at base 2026110609; fresh real data at 20 seeds,
   197	  base 2026110610.
   198	* HD passes when **all three hosts** meet both of these:
   199	  * (iii): the Wilson upper bound is at most .0936 on V_null, V_worse and V_null_dep_r5.
   200	  * (ii): at least 3 of 5 Holm-significant improvements.
   201	* Scope: the iid / weakly dependent conditional null. The rho >= .8 failure is a known out-of-scope limitation.
   202	
   203	## Amendment 5 (2026-11-03): registered certificate-host RMM test (ARIS result-to-claim follow-up)
   204	The ARIS result-to-claim review (Codex) rated claim C1 "partial", because its scope to certificate hosts
   205	was post-hoc after HR failed. This amendment registers that narrower claim **before** running it, on fresh
   206	seeds and with a fourth certificate family: the strong gated re-port `gated_adaptive_range`
   207	(wmax_t = 1/q_t). The parameters are unchanged from the RMM line and nothing is tuned.
   208	
   209	* **Hosts.** gated_deployment, discern_style (corrected), SMCS+retain-incumbent and gated_adaptive_range,
   210	  each paired with its +RMM version.
   211	* **Seeds.** Synthetic: 100 at base 2026110611. Nulls: 1000 at base 2026110612, on V_null, V_worse,
   212	  V_null_dep_r8 and W_null_dep_r95.
   213	* **HC success.** HC succeeds only if all three conditions below hold for **all 4** hosts:
   214	  * (a) The paired 95% CI of J(h+RMM) - J(h), as a per-seed family mean over
   215	    {V_late_600, W_late_1000, V_better_a, V_better_b, V_better_c, V_near}, lies below 0.
   216	  * (b) On each of V_late_600 and W_late_1000, the one-sided paired test of h+RMM against the best
   217	    certified published comparator on the same seeds is significant. The Holm correction runs over
   218	    2 regimes x 4 hosts = 8 tests.
   219	  * (c) The iid-null first-promotion Wilson upper bound is <= .0936 on both V_null and V_worse.
   220	* **Co-primary reported endpoints, with the failure stated in advance.**
   221	  * Cost against the host on reversal (V_reversal_1000, W_reversal_700) and recurrence (V_recurrent).
   222	  * Promotion on the persistent nulls V_null_dep_r8 and W_null_dep_r95.
   223	  * The claim must state the result on these whatever it is.
===== docs/sota_sprint/fresh_real_protocol.md =====
     1	# SOTA sprint: fresh real-data external validation protocol
     2	
     3	Registered 2026-11-03 by the benchmarking/data agent. The datasets, pair specification, episode
     4	plan, label delays and analysis below were fixed **before any ShiftLab policy, candidate, or the
     5	screen runner touched these data**. Only the frozen incumbent/challenger models were fit (to
     6	produce the loss streams). The descriptive statistics in
     7	`data/sprint_fresh_losses/README.md` are evaluator-side and must not be used to tune, select or
     8	re-specify any policy, contract parameter or analysis choice.
     9	
    10	None of the five datasets has been used in ShiftLab before (previously used: airlines, bank,
    11	criteo, elec2, rialto, spam, NOAA weather, openml_ldpa, uci_occupancy). No substitutions were
    12	needed: all five suggested downloads succeeded (total about 35 MB).
    13	
    14	## Datasets
    15	
    16	All from the UCI Machine Learning Repository, license CC BY 4.0. Raw zips are kept under
    17	`data/raw/sprint_fresh/uci_<id>/raw.zip` (gitignored).
    18	
    19	| dataset | UCI id | URL | raw zip sha256 | span | rows n |
    20	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    21	| metro | 492 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/492/metro+interstate+traffic+volume.zip | b99aeabcbd6cc86f642da3a79d90883425798f58abd3b3302da2fa19dda73768 | 2012-10 to 2018-09, hourly | 48204 |
    22	| beijing_pm25 | 381 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/381/beijing+pm2+5+data.zip | 40cdd29c24918e855133dce280ff59779bb0b5852898e05bafcf422a4cc0a175 | 2010-01 to 2014-12, hourly | 41757 |
    23	| bike_hour | 275 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/275/bike+sharing+dataset.zip | b70182d0d0508e9abbb79306ce5c0cec34869000f8220175ac83d11dbe845401 | 2011-01 to 2012-12, hourly | 17379 |
    24	| appliances | 374 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/374/appliances+energy+prediction.zip | 2fccf354445d886e7917620b0195db1f3e3e34d5a067a93b844694a4c561255a | 2016-01 to 2016-05, 10-min | 19735 |
    25	| household_power | 235 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/235/individual+household+electric+power+consumption.zip | 9f84b46ade8a2d8e1286ec4b2b6c2987a45a755c59f263be3b3b3d10dfbda3ff | 2006-12 to 2010-11, 10-min means | 204950 |
    26	
    27	Derived loss caches (`data/sprint_fresh_losses/<dataset>.npz`, arrays `l0`, `l1` int8 (K, 2000),
    28	`starts`):
    29	
    30	| npz | file sha256 | array_content_sha256 |
    31	|---|---|---|
    32	| metro.npz | d2e44130d052f1e73a38c6e2b8d731d1c9bef5b955b0447c14c1a5998788bdb1 | fcffeb091836f64753acd1d59f1cb7d3cb9d698c2b1671c8899e584138bafb7d |
    33	| beijing_pm25.npz | b29ea4991e25abdd3160399e597d56ce08d786da7a6fd6a6439f4cdb5065a4aa | 5e162db728f5b3a87f98c8712bc2f188c39acb5c771a44d2b768cc8d33a6cdf0 |
    34	| bike_hour.npz | 6572177367e66927859e6a64e189ae41402bdc262d96f480490977f87c73fb66 | 9e00cebfd33996f4ed6f0db719e28d7f7a20c234d7696329a38c464b530655db |
    35	| appliances.npz | 03e7a8b93a55a4efe5a6cef5af25b16aa88f3b73c03bde6c2444037a543af734 | 4e86c780a1c16c3334517bee634e76aadd23bf2a4ce94213dbf1b5abbfdced51 |
    36	| household_power.npz | e72049ab25102e9192b4c1ad43c73ee2fffb66e6dcba30da27a96eee72c6a582 | e658a1f8e3a8ff6dcb9edc8e9594ea77207bb9a58dd36e32f905a251f7433983 |
    37	
    38	The file hash changes on rebuild because `np.savez` stamps zip entry times; verify rebuilds with
    39	`array_content_sha256` = sha256(l0.tobytes() + l1.tobytes() + starts.tobytes()).
    40	
    41	## Pair specification
    42	
    43	Mirrors `scripts/promising_directions/b3_real_data.py::build_fetched`; code in
    44	`scripts/sota_sprint/fresh_real.py` (sklearn 1.9.1, numpy 2.5.4, pandas 3.0.6).
    45	
    46	* Rows sorted chronologically; n rows after cleaning; n15 = int(0.15n), n30 = int(0.30n).
    47	* Label y = 1[target > median of target on rows [0, n30)].
    48	* Train-only preprocessing on rows [0, n30): StandardScaler (numeric) and
    49	  OneHotEncoder(min_frequency=20, handle_unknown="ignore") (categorical).
    50	* Incumbent pi_0: LogisticRegression(max_iter=300, C=1.0) on rows [0, n15).
    51	* Challenger pi_1: HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=100, max_depth=4, learning_rate=0.1,
    52	  random_state=0) on rows [0, n30).
    53	* Losses l_k = 1[pi_k(x) != y]. Both are observed for every row once the label arrives, so the
    54	  paired loss difference is identifiable without an off-policy design.
    55	* Per-dataset target, leakage drops and features are listed in `data/sprint_fresh_losses/README.md`.
    56	  Leakage drops: bike casual/registered; household Global_intensity and Sub_metering_1-3.
    57	  Timestamps are dropped after deriving hour, weekday and month.
    58	
    59	## Episode plan
    60	
    61	K = min(20, floor((n - n30)/2000)) consecutive non-overlapping windows of T = 2000 rows starting
    62	at n30. `starts` in each npz gives absolute row indices.
    63	
    64	| dataset | n30 | K | last row used |
    65	|---|---|---|---|
    66	| metro | 14461 | 16 | 46461 |
    67	| beijing_pm25 | 12527 | 14 | 40527 |
    68	| bike_hour | 5213 | 6 | 17213 |
    69	| appliances | 5920 | 6 | 17920 |
    70	| household_power | 61485 | 20 | 101485 |
    71	
    72	Total 62 episodes. The household_power windows after row 101485 are not used.
    73	
    74	## Label delays (registered, in events; `DELAYS` in fresh_real.py)
    75	
    76	One day of natural time: metro 24, beijing_pm25 24, bike_hour 24, appliances 144,
    77	household_power 144. Metro has 7629 rows that share an hour with another row (one row per weather
    78	description), so 24 events is somewhat less than 24 hours there. Delays are fixed and in event
    79	order; no missing or out-of-order labels are simulated.
    80	
    81	## Analysis the lead will run
    82	
    83	* Contract: T = 2000, B = 200, c_q = .05, c_s = 5, label delay as registered above.
    84	* Policy seeds: 20 per episode at seed base 2026110604. Policy randomness is averaged within an
    85	  episode; the episode is the unit of inference.
    86	* Primary statistic per dataset and contrast: episode-level paired Delta J = J(candidate) -
    87	  J(comparator), mean over episodes with a 95% t-interval (df = K - 1). Also pooled over all 62
    88	  episodes, and reported per dataset.
    89	* Because episodes within a dataset are consecutive windows of one series, they are dependent and
    90	  the t-intervals are optimistic. Report, in addition: the fraction of episodes with Delta J < 0,
    91	  and a per-dataset two-sided exact sign test on episode Delta J (ties dropped).
    92	* Report promotion time and false promotions (switching on episodes where the challenger's
    93	  episode mean loss is not lower) as descriptive outcomes. Note that 56 of 62 episodes favour the
    94	  challenger, so these streams are mainly a test of cost of promoting a better challenger; harmful
    95	  switches are only possible on 6 episodes (5 household_power, 1 beijing_pm25).
    96	* Market or calendar dates are not causal change labels; no change times are inferred.
    97	
    98	## Status
    99	
   100	Smoke-level build only. No policy has been run on these streams as of registration.
===== docs/sota_sprint/benchmark_spec.md =====
     1	# SOTA sprint: frozen benchmark specification (Agent E)
     2	
     3	Registered 2026-11-03 on branch `research/sota-sprint-24` (from `origin/main` 2171689) before any
     4	sprint run. Code: [scripts/sota_sprint/bench.py](../../scripts/sota_sprint/bench.py).
     5	
     6	* Contract: T=2000, hard B=200, c_q=.05, c_s=5, alpha=.05, release-before-act, delay >= 1, common
     7	  simulator `scripts/promising_directions/sota/simulator.py`. J = loss + .05 labels + 5 switches.
     8	* Paired streams: synthetic seed s of regime R = `SeedSequence([seed_base, sha(R), s])`; every policy
     9	  replays the same stream with a private generator.
    10	* Development screening: seed base 2026110501 (unused before), 20 seeds per environment, environments
    11	  from `DEV_ENVS` only (Atlas v2 F0-F9 + sprint additions S_* + budget variants `@B100`, `@B400`).
    12	* Validation (registered later, before any validation run): fresh seed base, 100 paired seeds, the
    13	  Atlas v2 V_* regimes plus new instances; null safety additionally on >= 500 null seeds.
    14	* Null-safety bound: promotion rate <= .0936 on every null regime incl. rho=.8 and rho=.95 Markov
    15	  nulls (Atlas v2 Amendment 2). 20-seed rates are screening diagnostics, not evidence of safety.
    16	* Comparators: `CERTIFIED_PUBLISHED` (strongest certified class, chosen per regime on the same seeds),
    17	  `UNCERTIFIED_PUBLISHED`, internal earlier candidates (QSG, QSG-env8, FLQ, RMM, LRMD, P7s; never
    18	  counted as published SOTA), controls. Author-faithful status per method is in
    19	  `docs/promising_directions/sota/SOTA_IMPLEMENTATION_VALIDATION.md`; every row here is a
    20	  common-contract port unless stated.
    21	* Primary statistic: paired Delta J = J(candidate) - J(comparator), 95% t-interval over seeds.
    22	* Runner/analysis: `scripts/sota_sprint/screen.py`, `scripts/sota_sprint/analyze.py`.
===== docs/sota_sprint/REPORT.md =====
     1	# ShiftLab SOTA sprint: execution report
     2	
     3	**Date:** 2026-11-03.
     4	**Branch:** `research/sota-sprint-24`, from `origin/main` 2171689.
     5	**Protocol:** [benchmark_spec.md](benchmark_spec.md) and [validation_protocol.md](validation_protocol.md).
     6	The protocol was locked before validation and amended five times.
     7	* Amendments 1–3 were committed before any validation row was read.
     8	* Amendment 4 was registered afterwards and is labelled post-hoc.
     9	* Amendment 5 registers the certificate-host RMM claim before its fresh run. It follows from the ARIS
    10	  result-to-claim review.
    11	**Contract:** T=2000, hard B=200, c_q=.05, c_s=5, α=.05, common simulator, paired streams.
    12	
    13	## 1. Mandatory execution gate (brief §14A): MET
    14	
    15	* **Implementation and screening.** All 24 candidates (A1–A6, B1–B6, C1–C6, D1–D6) were implemented,
    16	  unit-tested and screened. There are 102 tests in `tests/sota_sprint`.
    17	  * Per family: 9–12 diagnostic environments, 20 paired seeds, a null or adverse environment, and an
    18	    environment outside the intended region.
    19	  * Each candidate has a mechanism ablation, saved raw records, cost, efficacy and safety metrics, and a
    20	    disposition.
    21	* **Mechanical audit from raw records** ([gate_audit.json](gate_audit.json),
    22	  `scripts/sota_sprint/gate_audit.py --root outputs/sota_sprint/gate`).
    23	  * 24/24 PASS.
    24	  * The audit is family-isolated: a candidate is credited only with runs from its own family's
    25	    directories. This follows the ARIS milestone-1 audit finding that names were reused across families.
    26	  * The re-screen was run at the committed revision. It reproduces 36,696 of 37,364 (98.2%) of the
    27	    agents' original dev rows exactly. The remainder came from intermediate code revisions.
    28	* **Ledger and leaderboard.** [candidates.json](candidates.json) and [leaderboard.json](leaderboard.json)
    29	  hold the per-candidate dispositions. [leaderboard_views.md](leaderboard_views.md) has the seven ranked
    30	  views (§12), with development and validated results kept apart.
    31	* **Bounded follow-up loop (§14C).** It ran: Amendment 5 targets the evidence gap that result-to-claim
    32	  named first.
    33	* **Phase 7 combinations.**
    34	  * Built and screened: X_A5B5, X_A6B5, X_A5C, X_D4A1, X_D6A1:mp and X_D6A1:cams
    35	    ([combos/README.md](combos/README.md)).
    36	  * Only X_A5C met the advance rule.
    37	  * Not built, with reasons recorded: A2+C3, B5+C5, B6+A1 and D6+C2.
    38	
    39	| family | dispositions (development) |
    40	|---|---|
    41	| A, dependence-aware certification | A1, A5 promising (valid for any ρ); A2, A3 fast but unsafe; A4 valid, no power; A6 = A5 |
    42	| B, recovery | B2 promising vs its host (reversal); B1, B3–B6 negative |
    43	| C, acquisition economics | C2, C6 weak (label saving only; did not replicate); C1, C3–C5 negative |
    44	| D, safety for fast selectors | D1, D2, D6 promising (null control; transfer across 3 hosts); D3–D5 negative |
    45	
    46	## 2. Validation (fresh seeds; registered hypotheses)
    47	
    48	The full tables are in [validation/validation_results.md](validation/validation_results.md),
    49	[validation/real_results.md](validation/real_results.md) and
    50	[validation/transfer_results.md](validation/transfer_results.md).
    51	
    52	* **Seeds:** 100 per regime over 21 regimes (base 2026110601); 1000 per null over 7 in-class nulls and
    53	  2 out-of-class nulls.
    54	* **Real data:** 5 fresh UCI chronological datasets with 62 episodes. They were registered (aeb2d4f)
    55	  before any policy run. The 9 previously exposed streams are reported descriptively.
    56	
    57	| registered test | result |
    58	|---|---|
    59	| H0, the Atlas v2 stop rule (A5b vs Xu / tuned pacing; CI<0 on ≥ 4/6) | **FAIL** (1/6) |
    60	| H1, A5b class-scoped reliability, part 1 (safety, Wilson upper bound, 7 in-class nulls) | PASS (≤ .005) |
    61	| H1, part 2 (each certified comparator fails a null or is cost-dominated) | PASS (11/11) |
    62	| H1, part 3 (non-inferiority within +5 on ≥ 4/5 iid alternatives) | **FAIL** (1/5) |
    63	| H2, cost superiority with safety (A5b, A1b, D1, D2, C2, C6, X_A5C, B2, B5) | **FAIL** for all; D2 is cheaper (4/5 Holm) but unsafe at ρ ≥ .8 |
    64	| H4, combinations | only X_A5C advanced; its late-regime gain vs A5b is −0.5 / −0.9 |
    65	| HR (Amendment 3), RMM transfer to ≥ 4/5 hosts | **FAIL** (3/5; all 3 certificate hosts pass) |
    66	| HD (Amendment 4, post-hoc), deploy-gate transfer to 3/3 hosts | **FAIL** (Holm-significant 0/2/2 of 5; all safe on iid nulls) |
    67	| **HC (Amendment 5), RMM on 4 certificate hosts: family < host, late vs best certified (Holm over 8), iid safety, all 4/4** | **PASS** (family −14.5 to −47.2; late −14.6 to −30.3 vs Xu, all Holm p < 1e-6; reversal +3 to +35 for 3 of 4 hosts; ρ=.8 null .12–.18, ρ=.95 null ≈ .71, as declared) |
    68	
    69	## 3. Best mechanisms and the evidence for them
    70	
    71	1. **RMM on certificate hosts (gated, corrected DISCERN, SMCS; the registered Amendment 5 adds the strong gated re-port).** On fresh seeds (Amendment 5, [amend5_results.md](validation/amend5_results.md)) the results replicate, with family −41.4, −47.2, −14.5 and −32.1.
    72	   * Family cost vs host: −44.2, −47.3 and −13.3.
    73	   * Late improvement vs the best certified published method (Xu): −15 to −27 for every host.
    74	   * Independent-null safety holds.
    75	   * Attribution: the restart mixture contributes −2.4 to −5.5 and continued acquisition −8 to −11.
    76	   * Losses: reversal and recurrence +20 to +37; ρ=.8 null promotion .15.
    77	2. **A5b (gap-indexed sequential universal inference certificate plus adaptive density).**
    78	   * The only powered certified method that stays within the bound for every ρ ≤ .95. It also holds on
    79	     two out-of-class nulls (.048, .018).
    80	   * Stationary cost is within +7.4 of the cheapest unsafe certified port.
    81	   * It fails on real time-series data (+61 to +257). Measured real persistence is ρ̂ .4–.98.
    82	3. **Deploy gate on fast selectors.**
    83	   * Native null promotion of 64–100% falls to about .015 on the three hosts.
    84	   * D2 (CAMS) beats the best certified method on 4/5 fresh real datasets.
    85	   * Its synthetic superiority did not replicate on new seeds.
    86	
    87	## 4. Research verdicts
    88	
    89	* **SOTA: `SOTA_NOT_MET`** under the brief's full success gate (§14B).
    90	  * The registered, fresh-seed **scoped synthetic SOTA** is now established: RMM attached to four
    91	    certificate hosts beats the strongest certified published comparator (Xu) on late-improvement
    92	    regimes by 15–30 per 2000 events (HC PASS).
    93	  * Gate items 1–4 hold for that scope: advantage, iid-null safety, fresh reproducibility, ablations.
    94	  * Item 5 (credible real-world relevance) is **not met**. The fresh real streams contain no
    95	    late-improvement episode. On them, gated+RMM beats its own host by 48–156 but is mixed against
    96	    the best certified method (2 wins, 3 losses).
    97	  * Item 6 (result-to-claim) is partial; see §5.
    98	  * The method also loses on reversals and fails persistent nulls, as declared in advance.
    99	* **Main-track candidates:** see the [publication register](publication_register.md) and §5.
   100	
   101	## 5. Independent reviews
   102	
   103	* **Milestones 1–2.** [review_milestone12.md](review_milestone12.md), by a native Claude reviewer. Its
   104	  objections O1–O14 were handled by Amendment 2 before any validation row was read.
   105	* **ARIS `experiment-audit`, milestone 1.** [aris/m1_EXPERIMENT_AUDIT.md](aris/m1_EXPERIMENT_AUDIT.md).
   106	  Run by Codex `gpt-reserve`, a different model family. Verdict FAIL on gate-audit name pooling, now
   107	  fixed.
   108	* **ARIS `experiment-audit` and `result-to-claim`, milestone 3.** In [aris/](aris/); see the addendum
   109	  below.
   110	* **Novelty.** [novelty_audit.md](novelty_audit.md).
   111	
   112	## 6. Reproduction
   113	
   114	    uv run pytest -q tests/sota_sprint
   115	    ./docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/run_validation.sh      # synthetic, nulls, B5, real, QCE, out-of-class
   116	    ./docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/run_rmm_transfer.sh    # Amendments 3 and 4
   117	    ./docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/run_gate.sh            # family-isolated gate re-screen
   118	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_validation.py
   119	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_real.py
   120	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_transfer.py
   121	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/gate_audit.py --root outputs/sota_sprint/gate
   122	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/leaderboard_views.py
   123	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/fresh_real.py   # rebuilds the fresh loss caches from UCI
   124	
   125	The run scripts are committed in `docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/` (run from the repo root; `run_gate.sh` reads its policy/regime lists from `outputs/sota_sprint/`, copies of which are alongside); each run also records its argv in
   126	its `manifest.json`. Raw records stay local.
   127	
   128	## 7. Limitations
   129	
   130	* Every comparator is a common-contract port, not author code.
   131	* Development used 20 seeds.
   132	* Real episodes are consecutive windows, so their CIs are optimistic.
   133	* Four of the five part-3 regimes were exposed in Atlas v2, with other seeds.
   134	* Family C held-out seeds shared the null-check base.
   135	* The fresh real streams contain almost no harmful-switch episodes (6/62), so they mostly measure
   136	  promotion cost.
   137	* PaperBanana was not used; no figures were produced.
   138	* `validation_spec.VAL_SEEDS` and `NULL_SEEDS` duplicate literals in the run scripts. This drift risk
   139	  was flagged by the ARIS audit; the values agree (100 and 1000).
===== docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md =====
     1	# Publication Opportunity Register (SOTA sprint, 2026-11-03)
     2	
     3	> **Claim wording after the ARIS result-to-claim review.** All three claims were rated *partial*, with
     4	> high confidence. The reviewer's revised wordings ([aris/m3_RESULT_TO_CLAIM.md](aris/m3_RESULT_TO_CLAIM.md))
     5	> are the claims of record. P2 is a validity-power trade-off shown empirically on the tested Markov
     6	> family; it is not a theorem. P1 is a scoped, synthetic-only method result. Calling either one a
     7	> "main-track candidate" is a publication judgement, not an experimental result.
     8	
     9	This register follows the publishability addendum. Main-track contribution potential (A) and SOTA performance (B) are assessed separately.
    10	
    11	All numbers come from the sprint validation files in [validation/](validation/). Status uses three values: PROMISING, NEEDS EVIDENCE and NOT SUPPORTED. The result-to-claim review in [aris/](aris/) may revise these.
    12	
    13	---
    14	
    15	## P1. A restart-mixture label-clock monitor that transfers across certified deployment methods (RMM)
    16	
    17	* **Research question.** Certified (anytime-valid) deployment gates stop measuring once they reach a verdict, and they dilute late evidence with early evidence. Can a single attachable module fix late-improvement latency for any certificate host?
    18	* **Insight / mechanism.**
    19	  * The module runs a Shiryaev-Roberts-style restart mixture of label-clock e-processes. It works on query-conditional, unweighted outcomes.
    20	  * It keeps acquiring labels after the host's verdict.
    21	  * When it alarms, it switches deployment and restarts the host. The alpha is split between host and detector.
    22	* **Closest prior art.**
    23	  * E-detectors (Shin, Ramdas and Rinaldo); Saha and Ramdas.
    24	  * Data-efficient quickest change detection (Banerjee and Veeravalli).
    25	  * The certified hosts: Karampatziakis et al. 2021, DISCERN, SMCS.
    26	* **Exact difference.** The module attaches to an arbitrary certified deployment host, on the label clock, under a shared hard label budget. It continues acquiring after the verdict and restarts the host. No paper evaluates restart detectors as a deployment add-on across certificate families.
    27	* **Evidence.** All results are on fresh seeds, after the DISCERN `/4` fix ([transfer_results.md](validation/transfer_results.md), [rmm_certified_hosts.txt](validation/rmm_certified_hosts.txt)).
    28	  * Host+RMM minus host on the registered family:
    29	    * gated −44.2 [−46.9, −41.4]
    30	    * corrected DISCERN −47.3 [−49.8, −44.8]
    31	    * SMCS −13.3 [−16.0, −10.6]
    32	  * Independent-null first-promotion safety holds on all three hosts (Wilson upper bound ≤ .0936 at 1000 seeds).
    33	  * Late improvement vs the best certified published method (Xu) is −15 to −27 per episode for every certified host, on V_late_600 and W_late_1000.
    34	  * Family mean vs strong baselines (gated+front, QCE+front, P7s, A5b) is −2.4 to −7.3.
    35	  * Ablations: the restart mixture contributes −2.4 to −5.5 and continued acquisition −8 to −11.
    36	* **Adverse results.**
    37	  * Registered HR fails at 3/5 hosts. Model Picker and CAMS have no certificate for RMM to improve: their null promotion is .94 to .95 with or without RMM.
    38	  * Reversal and recurrence cost +20 to +37 vs the best certified method.
    39	  * Strong stationary improvement costs +9 to +34 vs front-loaded EB.
    40	  * The ρ=.8 Markov null gives .15 and ρ=.95 gives .66 to .70.
    41	  * On fresh real data RMM is much better than its host (gated: −48 to −156) but mixed vs the best certified method (2 wins, 3 losses). The fresh streams contain no late-improvement episode.
    42	* **Registered replication (Amendment 5, fresh seeds, 4 certificate hosts including the strong gated re-port): HC PASS.**
    43	  * Family vs host: −14.5 to −47.2.
    44	  * Late regimes vs Xu: −14.6 to −30.3, all Holm-significant over 8 tests.
    45	  * iid-null safety holds.
    46	  * Reversal is +3 to +35 for 3 of 4 hosts, and ρ=.8/.95 null promotion is .12–.18 / ≈.71, as declared in advance.
    47	  * This turns the earlier post-hoc certificate-host scope into a registered result.
    48	* **Deviation to disclose.** Restricting the transfer claim to certificate hosts was decided after the registered 5-host HR failed. It is therefore a post-hoc scope, even though RMM's design rationale (adding monitoring to a certificate) predates it.
    49	* **Necessary additional evidence.**
    50	  1. A registered fresh test restricted to certificate hosts, adding a fourth certificate family (e.g. choe_ramdas_eb or gated_adaptive_range).
    51	  2. Real streams with a genuine within-stream improvement, e.g. a challenger retrained on recent data that overtakes the incumbent.
    52	  3. Fixing or bounding the reversal cost, with B2-style certified rollback.
    53	  4. A formal statement of the joint error of promote then rollback.
    54	* **Positioning.** A transferable extension (addendum type 1) and failure-guided design (type 4), with a scoped SOTA in the late-improvement regime (type 3).
    55	* **(A) Main-track potential:** moderate. Novelty is incremental (composition of e-detectors). The transfer across three independent certificate families plus the latency diagnosis gives it a coherent story.
    56	* **(B) SOTA:** scoped, synthetic only. Late improvement beats the strongest certified published method by 15 to 27; nothing broader.
    57	* **Assessment: PROMISING.** Item 1 is done (Amendment 5 PASS). Item 2, real late-improvement streams, is the remaining blocker for real-world relevance.
    58	
    59	## P2. Certified deployment under serial dependence: failure map and an impossibility-flavoured trade-off (Failure Atlas plus persistence study)
    60	
    61	* **Research question.** Do anytime-valid deployment gates keep their false-promotion guarantee when label outcomes are serially dependent, as they are in real time-series streams? What does a dependence-robust certificate cost?
    62	* **Main finding.**
    63	  * Every certified published port that has power exceeds the .0936 bound on a persistent marginal null. Run at 1000 seeds each:
    64	    * Xu: ρ=.9 .117, ρ=.95 .239
    65	    * SMCS: ρ=.8 .097, ρ=.95 .350
    66	    * gated_adaptive_range: ρ=.95 .112
    67	    * DISCERN floor: ρ=.95 .110
    68	    * Choe–Ramdas-style EB: ρ=.95 .213, front-loaded ρ=.8 .116
    69	    * QCE-betting: ρ=.95 .232
    70	  * The two that pass, original gated deployment and corrected DISCERN, almost never promote. A5b cost-dominates them on all 6 stationary alternatives.
    71	  * A gap-indexed sequential-universal-inference certificate (A1b/A5b) holds the bound for every ρ ≤ .95. It also holds on two out-of-class nulls (.048, .018). Its stationary cost is within +7.4 of the cheapest unsafe certified method.
    72	  * But it loses all power on real time-series streams. Fresh real data: +61 to +257 vs the best certified method. On metro it costs +99 with 0/16 episodes better, even though the challenger error is .047 vs .36.
    73	  * The mechanism is measured persistence: ρ̂ of .4 to .8 on the fresh streams, and up to .98 on exposed streams.
    74	  * This sits alongside the shared certification-latency diagnosis (M1, uniform pacing under a binding budget) and the churn of uncertified selectors (M2, 64 to 100% null promotion).
    75	* **Closest prior art.**
    76	  * Choe and Ramdas 2024; Henzi and Ziegel 2022. These control the time-averaged *conditional* mean, so a promotion in a persistent run is correct for their null.
    77	  * Universal inference (Wasserman, Ramdas and Balakrishnan 2020).
    78	  * Markov e-detectors.
    79	* **Exact difference.**
    80	  * The atlas measures marginal false promotion of deployment gates under controlled persistence, on one common budgeted, delayed simulator, against 13 published methods. It also shows the conditional-vs-marginal null distinction has decision consequences.
    81	  * A gap-indexed Markov UI certificate for actively queried outcomes; the novelty audit found no prior use of it.
    82	* **Necessary additional evidence.**
    83	  1. A short formal argument that, under unknown persistence, any test valid for all ρ ≤ 1 needs evidence that grows with run changes rather than with labels. This would turn the trade-off into a lower bound.
    84	  2. Real streams with a known null, e.g. two equally accurate retrained models, to measure real false-promotion rates.
    85	  3. Author-code comparators where available.
    86	* **Positioning.** A new evaluation methodology plus a substantive empirical finding (addendum types 5 and 6), possibly a datasets-and-benchmarks track.
    87	* **(A) Main-track potential:** moderate to good as an empirical paper, if the lower-bound argument holds.
    88	* **(B) SOTA:** A5b is the lowest-cost method among those that hold the bound at ρ ≤ .95 on synthetic data. It is not SOTA on real data.
    89	* **Assessment: PROMISING (needs evidence item 1).**
    90	
    91	## P3. A shared deploy-or-abstain gate for fast online model selectors (Family D)
    92	
    93	* **Research question.** Can fast uncertified selectors (Model Picker, CAMS, Model Selector) keep most of their economy once an anytime certificate is required before deployment?
    94	* **Mechanism.** The host ranks and queries. Promotion requires a started-mixture e-process on query-conditional outcomes. Gate queries are added only while the host is undecided.
    95	* **Evidence.**
    96	  * Native null promotion of 64 to 100% falls to .014 to .016 on V_null for all three hosts at 1000 seeds.
    97	  * Fresh real data, D2 (CAMS) vs the best certified method:
    98	    * beijing −33.2 (13/14 episodes)
    99	    * bike −6.1 (5/6)
   100	    * metro −2.7 (14/16)
   101	    * household −2.8 (11/20)
   102	    * appliances +1.4
   103	* **Adverse results.**
   104	  * Registered HD fails. Holm-significant wins vs the best certified method number 0, 2 and 2 of 5.
   105	  * D2's first-validation 4/5 did not replicate on new seeds.
   106	  * ρ=.8 null promotion is .20 to .23.
   107	  * Real gains are consistent only on the CAMS host.
   108	* **Closest prior art.** Composition of published gates (Karampatziakis et al. 2021) with published selectors. The novelty audit judged it composition.
   109	* **(A) Main-track potential:** low as a method. It is useful as the M2 benchmark finding inside P2.
   110	* **(B) SOTA:** not established.
   111	* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE.** Unsupported as a standalone contribution pending additional evidence (ARIS result-to-claim wording).
   112	
   113	## P4. Certified rollback (B2/B5)
   114	
   115	* **Evidence.** Reversal cost vs host: B2 −8.9 and −8.4; B5 −12.0 and −17.8 (fresh seeds).
   116	* **Adverse results.**
   117	  * One host only, in the RMM line, which fails ρ=.8.
   118	  * Reversal is still +20 or more vs the best certified method (discern_style), because that method never promotes.
   119	* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE.** Unsupported as a standalone contribution pending additional evidence; best used as P1's fix for reversal cost.
   120	
   121	## P5. FLQ/QCE/QSG front-loading, adaptive density, shadow pricing (A2, A3, C1 to C6)
   122	
   123	* **Evidence.** Earlier Atlas v2 work: stationary gains bought with dependence-safety failure (QSG at ρ=.95: .696). The C family gives no replicated gain; C2's development gain did not replicate.
   124	* **Assessment: NOT SUPPORTED** as contributions. Kept as negative results within P2.
   125	
   126	## P6. LRMD (standalone restart monitor)
   127	
   128	* **Evidence.** Earlier registered null-safety failure. In the transfer run it is cheaper than gated+RMM on the family (+5.0 for gated+RMM vs LRMD), but its nulls were not re-tested here.
   129	* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE.** Unsupported as a standalone contribution pending a fresh 1000-seed null run.
   130	
   131	---
   132	
   133	## Ranked recommendation for paper development
   134	
   135	1. **P2** as a benchmark/empirical paper: certified deployment under dependence. It has the broadest finding, every number is on fresh seeds, and its real-data relevance was measured.
   136	2. **P1** as a method paper on transferable restart monitoring for certified deployment. Run its two missing experiments first.
   137	3. Fold P3 to P5 into P2 as findings and negative results.
